---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import IconButtonDemo from "../../../components/IconButtonDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";
import { componentExamples } from "../../../data/snippets";
import { iconKeyframesCss, iconKeyframesExample } from "../../../utils/icon-keyframes";

const { sizesExample, motionExample } = componentExamples["icon-button"];
const code = "mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4";
---

<ComponentPage slug="icon-button">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    It is the Button with a square size, so variants, loading and disabled work the same way.
  </Fragment>

  <IconButtonDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      An icon has no text, so <code>aria-label</code> is the button's only accessible name. It is
      required: leaving it out is a TypeScript error. Describe the action, such as "Close dialog",
      not the picture, such as "Cross".
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The icon is wrapped in an <code>aria-hidden</code> span, so an SVG title or label never adds
      to the name. When there is room for a visible label, prefer a Button with text: everyone can
      read it, not only screen reader users.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="examples">
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Sizes</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      <code>icon-sm</code>, <code>icon</code> (the default) and <code>icon-lg</code> are fully
      rounded squares of 32, 40 and 48px, all above the 24px minimum target. An icon without its own
      <code>size-*</code> class scales with the button. The same sizes work on
      <code>Button</code> and on <code>buttonVariants()</code>.
    </p>
    <div class={code}>
      <Code code={sizesExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Loading</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      While <code>loading</code>, the spinner replaces the icon and the button keeps its name and
      focus. As with the Button, announce progress from a <code>role="status"</code> region outside
      it.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Motion</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      <code>motion</code> gives the icon a gesture. It plays once each time the pointer arrives or
      keyboard focus lands on the button, never loops, and only runs when the user has not asked for
      reduced motion. The colors and the focus ring do not move.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <code>rotate</code>: a close cross turns a quarter while hovered or focused.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>spin</code>: a refresh arrow turns once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>wiggle</code>: a trash can shakes once. Mark its lid with <code>data-lid</code> and
        the lid lifts first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>swing</code>: a bell swings from the top, a little less on every pass.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>nudge-x</code>, <code>nudge-up</code> and <code>nudge-diagonal</code>: an arrow, a
        send or an external-link icon moves out and back. Forward follows the reading direction.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>pop</code>: a like grows and settles, and pops again when pressed.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class={code}>
      <Code code={motionExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The gestures are CSS keyframes, so installing the button with the CLI also adds them to your
      CSS file. It adds only these, with a <code>buril-icon-</code> prefix, and leaves your tokens
      and variants alone:
    </p>
    <div class={code}>
      <Code code={iconKeyframesExample()} lang="css" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="install-notes">
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      <code>IconButton</code> ships in the same registry item as the Button, in
      <code>components/ui/button.tsx</code>, so installing either one gives you both.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      The CLI also adds the <code>motion</code> keyframes to your CSS. If you copy the file by hand,
      paste them into your CSS too; without them the icons stay still.
    </p>
    <div class={code}>
      <Code code={iconKeyframesCss()} lang="css" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</ComponentPage>
